claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:

> On Wed, 14 Oct 2026 15:08:08 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
>> Also what would be a good way to represent approved / rejected? I have a
>> few ideas:
>>
>> 1. approved: yes or approved: no
>> 2. state: approved or state: rejected
>> 3. approved (can git parse that? maybe I do approved: with an optional
>> message)
>> 4. rejected: <reason>
>
> I recommend two keys, "Approved:" and "Rejected:", each with an
> optional free-text value. That is your 3 with the colon, plus your 4:
>
>     Approved:
>     Approved: once the doas question is settled
>     Rejected: the hook belongs on the mail branch

Okay I like this. One note: the "Approved: once the doas question is
settled" is a bad example. It's additional a condition to this, and the
whole point is that it's deterministic. If there's an "Approved:"
trailer, it's approved. So really, most of them are going to be empty -
although it could be a place to add a note of gratitude, or indicate
ideas for follow up, or whatever. But this idea there that it's
expressing future approval - nope. Approved: means that it's approved
as-is.

> Why two keys rather than the others:
>
> - The key is the state, and the value is free for the reason. A
>   rejection needs a reason more than anything else, and option 2 has
>   no room for one.
> - Option 1 can say things we would have to define. "approved: no"
>   could mean rejected, or it could mean that an earlier approval is
>   withdrawn. "approved: maybe" means nothing. With two keys there is
>   no value to validate.
> - Option 2 extends more easily, since one key could later take
>   needs-revision or applied. But a typo in the value ("State:
>   aproved") is ignored without a word. A typo in a key is ignored
>   too, so neither form protects against that. I would add a key when
>   a new state is needed.
> - "Latest wins" stays simple. The hook takes the last Approved: or
>   Rejected: on a given target, ordered by Date, and tags it approved
>   or rejected. The other tag comes off.
>
> I left out "-by". In the kernel and FreeBSD, Approved-by names the
> person, but here the From already names who wrote it. The value is
> then free for the reason, and that keeps it apart from Superseded-by.

That works. I had been thinking that "Approved:" would look funny, but
I'll get used to it, and the functionality is good.

>> I think superseded-by is important as its own because it establishes a
>> link. Approval / rejection is a bit different though.
>
> Agreed. Superseded-by points at another message, and Approved: and
> Rejected: point at nothing, because their target is the message they
> reply to. The series in "[PATCH 0/3] notmuch: Tag superseded series
> and hide them" [1] already works that way for Superseded-by: the hook
> tags the thread the reply sits in, and it does not use the value.
>
> That leaves two choices for you before I post a series:
>
> 1. Scope. As posted, the superseded hook tags the whole thread. For
>    approved and rejected I proposed that a reply to patch N/M tag only
>    that patch, and that a reply to the cover letter or a lone patch
>    tag the whole thread. That needs the hook to tag the In-Reply-To
>    target rather than the thread. Do you want per-patch tags, or is
>    per-thread enough for now?

Let's do approved, superseded, rejected at the thread level for the time
being. If I need to get more granular, I will.

> 2. Hiding. Should rejected join superseded in search.exclude_tags?
>    I would hide rejected and leave approved visible until it is
>    committed, since approved work still needs you to apply it.

No, I'll manually filter them for the time being. Once it's
demonstrating reliable value I can config notmuch to hide automatically.

> The withdrawn todo patch from my last reply, "[PATCH] todo: Send as
> Pat through lab-mail later" [2], would then take "Rejected: withdrawn,
> scope moved to <subject>" rather than a Superseded-by.
>
